---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// How the data moves, drawn as a course: each stop is a timing mat.
const flow = [
  { mat: '01', name: 'Your watch', note: 'Records every session. Mine is a Garmin Fenix 9 Pro.', color: 'var(--swim)' },
  { mat: '02', name: 'Strava + Intervals.icu', note: 'The watch app syncs each activity to both, automatically.', color: 'var(--bike)' },
  { mat: '03', name: 'Claude', note: 'Reads your training from Strava and your recovery from Intervals.icu.', color: 'var(--run)' },
  { mat: '04', name: 'Intervals.icu calendar', note: 'Claude writes the week’s workouts onto it.', color: 'var(--trans)' },
  { mat: 'Fin', name: 'Back on your watch', note: 'Intervals.icu sends each planned workout to the watch.', color: 'var(--accent)' },
];

const levels = [
  { tag: 'Level 1', time: '10 min', what: 'Strava connected to Claude. Enough to start.' },
  { tag: 'Level 2', time: '+20 min', what: 'Recovery data (sleep, HRV, resting heart rate) from Intervals.icu.' },
  { tag: 'Level 3', time: '+10 min', what: 'Workouts land on your watch without retyping them.' },
];
---
<Base title="Setup: connect your data to Claude" description="Step-by-step instructions for the setup from my videos: connect Strava to Claude, add recovery data from Intervals.icu, and get the plan onto your watch. Works with any watch and any sport.">
  <article class="wrap section setup stack">
    <p class="label">Setup · the instructions from my videos</p>
    <h1>Connect your data to Claude</h1>
    <p class="lede muted">
      If you came here from a video, this is the setup I use to have Claude coach my training. It reads what I actually
      did, plans the next week from it, and puts the workouts on my watch. I use a Garmin and do triathlon, but every
      step has a version for other watches and other sports.
    </p>

    <section class="stack" aria-labelledby="flow-heading">
      <h2 id="flow-heading">How it fits together</h2>
      <ol class="flow" role="list">
        {flow.map((stop) => (
          <li style={`--leg: ${stop.color}`}>
            <span class="mat mono">{stop.mat}</span>
            <strong>{stop.name}</strong>
            <span class="muted">{stop.note}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="stack" aria-labelledby="levels-heading">
      <h2 id="levels-heading">Pick how far to go</h2>
      <p>
        You don’t need all of it. Level 1 is the whole idea: a coach that sees your real training. Add the other two when
        you want them.
      </p>
      <ul class="levels" role="list">
        {levels.map((l) => (
          <li>
            <span class="mono tag">{l.tag}</span>
            <span class="mono time">{l.time}</span>
            <span>{l.what}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="leg stack" id="level-1" style="--leg: var(--swim)" aria-labelledby="l1">
      <p class="label">Level 1</p>
      <h2 id="l1">Strava, connected to Claude</h2>
      <p class="muted">You need: a Claude account, a Strava account with a subscription (the Claude connector requires one), and a watch or phone that uploads to Strava.</p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Get your sessions into Strava.</strong> In your watch’s app (Garmin Connect, COROS, Apple Health, Wahoo
          and most others), turn on the Strava connection so every activity uploads on its own. Check that last week’s
          sessions are there.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write notes on your activities.</strong> Claude reads the title and description too. One line like
          “calves tight from mile 3” or “skipped the last interval, work stress” tells it more than the numbers do.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Connect Strava to Claude.</strong> In Claude, open Settings, then Connectors, find Strava and connect
          it. Strava asks you to approve access. Claude can read your data but can’t edit or post anything.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Set up the coach.</strong> Make a Claude Project for your training and run the prompts from <a href="/guide/">the
          guide</a>, starting with the athlete profile. That’s where you give it your goal, your
          history and your limits.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Test it.</strong> Ask: “Look at my last four weeks on Strava. What did I actually do, and what stands
          out?” If it lists real sessions with real dates, you’re connected.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="leg stack" id="level-2" style="--leg: var(--bike)" aria-labelledby="l2">
      <p class="label">Level 2 · optional</p>
      <h2 id="l2">Recovery data from Intervals.icu</h2>
      <p>
        Strava knows what you did. It doesn’t know how you slept. <a href="https://intervals.icu">Intervals.icu</a> (free)
        pulls sleep, HRV and resting heart rate from your watch, and that’s what lets Claude say “you’re run down, swap
        tomorrow for something easy” before you feel it.
      </p>
      <p class="muted">
        There’s no Claude connector for Intervals.icu, so Claude reads it through its API with a key. This part is more
        technical. I do it in Claude Code, where the key lives in the environment settings instead of the chat.
      </p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Make an Intervals.icu account</strong> and connect your watch under Settings. Give it a few days to fill
          in history. Wellness numbers only show up if your watch tracks sleep.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Get your API key.</strong> In Intervals.icu Settings, under Developer Settings, generate an API key and
          note your athlete ID (it starts with “i”).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Store the key where Claude can use it without seeing it in a message.</strong> In Claude Code on the
          web, add it to your cloud environment’s settings as an environment variable. I use <code>INTERVALS_API_KEY</code> and <code>INTERVALS_ATHLETE_ID</code>. Then tell Claude the variable names, not
          the values.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Set the rules.</strong> Tell Claude to only read from the API, except for adding planned workouts in
          Level 3, and never to change or delete your activities.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <aside class="rule">
        <p class="mono label">Rule</p>
        <p>
          Never paste an API key into a chat, a video description or a screenshot. Anyone with the key can read your
          account. If one leaks, delete it in Intervals.icu and make a new one.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="leg stack" id="level-3" style="--leg: var(--run)" aria-labelledby="l3">
      <p class="label">Level 3 · optional</p>
      <h2 id="l3">The plan, on your watch</h2>
      <p>
        Claude doesn’t talk to your watch. It puts the workouts on your Intervals.icu calendar, and Intervals.icu sends
        them to the watch. That’s why the day’s session is just there when I press start.
      </p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Turn on workout upload.</strong> In Intervals.icu, open your watch connection in Settings and turn on
          uploading planned workouts. I use Garmin. Check the Intervals.icu <a href="https://intervals.icu/integrations/">integrations
          page</a> for whether yours supports it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ask for the week.</strong> Once you’ve agreed on the plan, ask Claude to add it to your Intervals.icu
          calendar. Have it write each workout as steps on their own lines (like <code>- Easy 35m</code>) so Intervals.icu
          turns them into a structured workout.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Check one before you rely on it.</strong> Open tomorrow’s workout in Intervals.icu, then sync your watch
          and find it there. If it shows up, the rest will too.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="muted">
        Skipping Levels 2 and 3? Ask Claude to write the workout out, then build it in your watch app’s workout builder.
        It takes two minutes a session.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="leg stack" id="manual" style="--leg: var(--trans)" aria-labelledby="manual-heading">
      <p class="label">Every level</p>
      <h2 id="manual-heading">Log what your watch can’t</h2>
      <p>
        I play water polo once or twice a week, and I don’t wear a watch for it. If it isn’t logged, my coach thinks I
        took a rest day. So after each practice I add a manual activity on Strava:
      </p>
      <ul class="steps">
        <li>Type “Workout”, titled with the sport (mine is “Polo”).</li>
        <li>How long you went, and Strava’s perceived exertion slider.</li>
        <li>One line in the description: anything that hurt, and whether it was drills or a game.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted">
        Log it on Strava directly. In my experience, manual activities added in Garmin Connect don’t sync over. The same
        trick covers a gym class, a pickup game, a hike, or a round of golf.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="stack" aria-labelledby="other-heading">
      <h2 id="other-heading">Different watch or sport?</h2>
      <dl class="other">
        <div>
          <dt>Apple Watch, COROS, Suunto, Wahoo, Polar</dt>
          <dd>Level 1 works the same as long as the watch app uploads to Strava. For Levels 2 and 3, check what your watch supports on the Intervals.icu integrations page.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>No watch</dt>
          <dd>Record on your phone with the Strava app, or log sessions by hand. Claude coaches from whatever you give it.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>A sport Strava doesn’t track well</dt>
          <dd>Skip the connector. Keep a simple spreadsheet or upload exports and scorecards to your Claude Project. <a href="/guide/">The guide</a> has examples for other sports, including golf.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="next stack" aria-labelledby="next-heading">
      <h2 id="next-heading">Next: give it a brief</h2>
      <p>
        A connection is just a pipe. The coaching comes from what you tell it: your goal, your history, your limits, and
        a weekly check-in. That’s the four prompts in the guide.
      </p>
      <p class="actions">
        <a class="button" href="/guide/">Get the prompts <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        <a class="button ghost" href="/progress/">See how it’s going for me</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  @layer components {
    .setup { --stack-gap: var(--space-m); max-inline-size: 48rem; }
    .setup section { --stack-gap: var(--space-s); padding-block-start: var(--space-m); }
    .lede { font-size: var(--step-1); line-height: 1.45; }
    code {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: 0.88em;
      padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
      border-radius: 4px;
      background: var(--surface-2);
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }

    /* The data path as a course: a line with a timing mat at each stop. */
    .flow { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin-block-start: var(--space-s); display: grid; }
    .flow li {
      position: relative;
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 3.25rem 1fr;
      column-gap: var(--space-s);
      padding-block: 0.6rem;
    }
    .flow li > :not(.mat) { grid-column: 2; }
    .flow li::before {
      content: "";
      position: absolute;
      inset-block: 0;
      inset-inline-start: calc(1.625rem - 1px);
      inline-size: 2px;
      background: repeating-linear-gradient(var(--leg) 0 6px, transparent 6px 10px);
    }
    .flow li:first-child::before { inset-block-start: 50%; }
    .flow li:last-child::before { inset-block-end: 50%; }
    .mat {
      position: relative;
      grid-row: span 2;
      align-self: center;
      display: grid;
      place-items: center;
      block-size: 2rem;
      border-radius: 4px;
      background: var(--surface-2);
      box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--leg);
      color: var(--leg);
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      text-transform: uppercase;
    }

    .levels { list-style: none; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 0.4rem; }
    .levels li {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: auto auto 1fr;
      align-items: baseline;
      gap: var(--space-s);
      padding: 0.7rem var(--space-s);
      background: var(--surface);
      border: 1px solid var(--line);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
    }
    .levels .tag { color: var(--accent); text-transform: uppercase; font-size: var(--step--1); }
    .levels .time { color: var(--ink-faint); font-size: var(--step--1); }
    @media (width < 32rem) {
      .levels li { grid-template-columns: auto 1fr; row-gap: 0.2rem; }
      .levels li > :last-child { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
    }

    .leg { border-inline-start: 3px solid var(--leg); padding-inline-start: var(--space-m); }
    .leg .label { color: var(--leg); }
    .steps { display: grid; gap: var(--space-s); padding-inline-start: 1.25em; }
    .steps li::marker { color: var(--ink-faint); font-family: var(--font-mono); }

    .rule {
      background: var(--accent-dim);
      border: 1px solid var(--accent);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      padding: var(--space-s) var(--space-m);
    }
    .rule .label { color: var(--accent); margin-block-end: 0.25em; }

    .other { display: grid; gap: var(--space-s); }
    .other div { padding: var(--space-s) var(--space-m); background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); }
    .other dt { font-weight: 700; }
    .other dd { margin: 0.25em 0 0; color: var(--ink-muted); }

    .actions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-s); }
  }
</style>
